\subsection*{Per-Feature Violin Plots}

Figure~\ref{fig:violin} shows the full distribution of each feature across five generation depths (0--4), pooled across all eight generators ($N$\,=\,37{,}058).
The violin plots confirm the DDR pattern identified in the main text: drifting features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) show progressive narrowing and location shift, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay) maintain approximately constant distributions across depths.
Entropy std exhibits intermediate behavior with modest location shift.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_violin.pdf}
\caption{Feature distributions by generation depth (violin plots, $N$\,=\,37{,}058). Drifting features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio: $|\rho| > 0.60$) show clear location shifts and narrowing with increasing depth. Shape features (entropy max, tail decay: $|\rho| < 0.10$) remain approximately stationary. Quartile lines within each violin indicate median and interquartile range.}
\label{fig:violin}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{DDR Trend Lines}

Figure~\ref{fig:ddr-trends} shows the per-feature depth trend (mean across all samples at each depth) with individual generator traces overlaid.
The consistent downward trajectories for drifting features across all generators confirm that DDR is a universal property of recursive generation, not specific to any single model.
Shape features exhibit flat trajectories regardless of generator, with near-zero $R^2$ for the linear fit.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_ddr_trends.pdf}
\caption{Per-feature depth trends. Black line: pooled mean ($\pm$1 std shading) across all generators. Colored traces: individual generators. Features are labeled by DDR category (drifting, intermediate, stationary). Spearman $\rho$ and linear $R^2$ are annotated per panel.}
\label{fig:ddr-trends}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{UMAP Embedding}

Figure~\ref{fig:umap} shows a 2D UMAP projection of the 7-feature space (7{,}500 stratified samples, StandardScaler normalization, $k$\,=\,30 neighbors, min\_dist\,=\,0.3).
Panel (a) reveals a depth gradient from gen-0 (green) through gen-4 (yellow), with progressive overlap at deeper generations consistent with the convergence that motivates the gen-2+ class collapse.
Panel (b) shows the 3-class view: gen-0 forms a separable cluster, while gen-1 and gen-2+ overlap substantially in feature space, explaining the classification difficulty at this boundary.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_umap.pdf}
\caption{UMAP 2D projection of the 7-feature space ($N$\,=\,7{,}500 stratified sample). (a) Colored by raw generation depth (0--4): a gradient structure emerges, with deeper depths converging. (b) Colored by 3-class label: gen-0 separates clearly; gen-1 and gen-2+ overlap, consistent with the high false-negative rate at the gen-1/gen-2+ boundary.}
\label{fig:umap}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{Confusion Matrix Visualization}

Figure~\ref{fig:confusion-heatmap} provides a heatmap visualization of the 3-class LOGO confusion matrix (complementing Table~\ref{tab:confusion} in Appendix~\ref{app:confusion}).
The row-normalized view in panel (b) highlights: (1) near-perfect gen-0 classification (99.4\% recall), (2) gen-1 confusion primarily with gen-2+ (18.8\%) rather than gen-0 (6.2\%), and (3) gen-2+ misclassification predominantly into gen-1 (23.3\%), consistent with DDR predictions that shallow recursive text has weak fingerprints.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{fig_appendix_confusion.pdf}
\caption{3-class LOGO confusion matrix ($N$\,=\,27{,}529). (a) Raw counts. (b) Row-normalized recall percentages. Gen-0 achieves near-perfect classification; the primary confusion axis is gen-1 $\leftrightarrow$ gen-2+.}
\label{fig:confusion-heatmap}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{Per-Generator Performance}

Figure~\ref{fig:per-gen} visualizes per-generator LOGO performance.
Panel (a) shows the 8-generator ranking: Pythia models (1B, 2.8B) achieve highest F1, while larger models (Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B) show degradation.
Dashed lines indicate pool-average performance.
The scale effect (larger models $\rightarrow$ lower F1) is consistent with higher-capacity models generating text statistically closer to human writing.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_per_gen.pdf}
\caption{Per-generator LOGO performance. (a) 8-generator binary and 3-class F1, sorted by binary F1. Dashed lines: pool average. (b) 6-generator LOGO detail heatmap showing the performance spread across model families.}
\label{fig:per-gen}
\end{figure*}

